WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:36.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. On a hot summer day in 1799, French Army engineers in occupied northern Egypt were sifting through the rubble of a centuries-old abandoned fortress. These soldiers had been tasked by their general, Napoleon Bonaparte, heard of him? To repair the fort for proper use. And as they were laying the foundation for the walls and barracks, someone made a startling discovery. That's producer Vivian Ley. They stumbled upon the Rosetta Stone, possibly the most famous chunk of rock in human history.
这里是《百分之九十九隐秘》（99% Invisible）。我是罗曼·马尔斯（Roman Mars）。1799年夏天的一个热天，在被占领的埃及北部，法国陆军的工程师们正在翻找一座废弃了几个世纪的古老堡垒的瓦砾。这些士兵受他们的将军拿破仑·波拿巴指派——听说过他吧？——去修复这座堡垒以便正常使用。正当他们为围墙和营房打地基时，有人有了一个惊人的发现。那是制作人薇薇安·莱（Vivian Ley）。他们偶然发现了罗夏石碑（译者注：原文如此，即罗塞塔石碑），这可能是人类历史上最著名的巨石。

2
00:00:36.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The reason it's famous and the reason you can see it when you go to the British Museum is that there was text in three languages. That's Joel Slemrod, professor of economics at the University of Michigan. The text on the Rosetta Stone had been inscribed with the same message in three different scripts — Greek, Egyptian Demotic Scripts, and Ancient Egyptian Hieroglyphics. Which at the time no one could decipher. Based on the first two scripts, researchers were eventually able to crack the code for how to finally read hieroglyphics, a writing system that hadn't been used for about 1,400
它之所以出名，而且你参观大英博物馆时能亲眼见到它，原因在于上面刻有三种语言的文字。说话的是密歇根大学经济学教授乔尔·斯莱姆罗德（Joel Slemrod）。罗塞塔石碑上的文字是用三种不同的书写系统刻着相同的信息——希腊语、埃及世俗体和古埃及象形文字。当时没有人能解读这些文字。借助前两种文字，研究人员最终得以破解密码，从而最终能够阅读象形文字，这是一种已经有大约1400年没有被使用过的书写系统。

3
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:47.000
years. Question, what was so important to write about? And the answer is, well, among other things, the Rosetta Stone describes a tax break given to the temple priests of ancient Egypt. So the Rosetta Stone helped us unlock the secret of hieroglyphics by talking about something in Texas. Taxes are essentially as old as human civilization, and they've been shaping it ever since. Historically, they've influenced everything from what our buildings look like, to what
问题来了，上面到底写了什么这么重要？答案是，除了别的之外，罗塞塔石碑描述的是给予古埃及神庙祭司的税收减免。所以，罗塞塔石碑通过谈论德克萨斯州（译者注：播客此处玩了一个双关语/口误，将taxes（税收）谐音成了Texas（德克萨斯州））的事情，帮我们解开了象形文字的秘密。税收基本上和人类文明一样古老，而且它从那时起一直在塑造着文明。从历史上看，它们影响了一切：从我们建筑的外观，到我们驾驶的汽车类型，再到我们选举谁当总统，甚至是否会爆发起义。历史表明，税法可以揭示很多关于一个社会的信息。如果你是一个美国人，那么得到的启示就是：报个人所得税简直糟透了。如果你在这一集发布的当天收听，也就是4月15日，也就是美国所谓的“纳税日”（tax day）。我们美国人往往和我们的税收体系有着相当紧张的关系。报税流程变得如此复杂，以至于普通人要花13个小时来

4
00:01:47.000 --> 00:02:23.000
kind of cars we drive, to who we elect as our president, to whether we revolt. History has shown that tax codes can reveal so much about a society. And if you're an American, the revelation is that filing our income taxes sucks. If you're listening to this episode on its release day, it is April 15th, aka tax day here in the United States. And we Americans tend to have a pretty fraught relationship with our tax system. The filing process has become so complicated that the average individual spends 13 hours
准备他们的个人所得税。当你恨不得自始至终都一拳砸向电脑屏幕时，这感觉会漫长一倍。53%的美国人对我们税收体系的复杂性感到非常烦恼。甚至有一项调查显示，它让三分之一的人“忍不住想哭”。据说连阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦也曾说过，世上最难理解的事情就是所得税。而他可是出了名的聪明。因此，在这个纳税季的最后一天，我们正在梳理近250年的美国税收历史，以弄清楚

5
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:57.000
preparing their income taxes. And that can feel twice as long when you want to put your fist through the computer screen the entire time. 53% of Americans are very bothered by the complexity of our tax system. And one survey even reported that it makes one in three people, quote, want to cry. Even Albert Einstein allegedly once said, the hardest thing in the world is to understand the income tax. And he was famously smart. So on this the final day of tax season, we are sifting through nearly 250 years of US tax history to figure out
事情怎么会变得如此该死地复杂。对税收感到愤怒是一种典型的美国体验。事实上，税收之怒是这个国家建立的一个重要原因。波士顿茶党事件是美国独立战争中的一个形成性事件。我们大多数人在初中时都学过，波士顿茶党事件是对提高茶叶税的不满而引发的反抗行为。但有趣的是，这完全是错的。实际上，事情并不是那样的。

6
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:32.000
how things got so damn complicated. Being mad about taxes is a quintessentially American experience. In fact, tax rage is a large reason why this country was created. The Boston Tea Party, a formative event in the American Revolution. Most of us learned in middle school that the Boston Tea Party was an act of defiance over an increase in taxes on tea. But fun fact, that's totally wrong. Actually though, that isn't what happened.
更接近真相的是，当时部分茶叶税其实降低了。当时，英国对茶叶征收极高的税率，以至于运往殖民地的茶叶中近三分之二都是为了避开成本而通过走私非法运入的。为了打击这种非法茶叶贸易，议会实际上降低了合法进口茶叶的税收。英国人的这些新提案似乎要剥夺一些通过走私茶叶维生的知名爱国者的生计。

7
00:03:32.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Closer to the truth was that some tax on tea had been decreased. At the time, Britain had been taxing tea at such a high rate that nearly two-thirds of tea imported to the colonies was smuggled in illegally to get around the cost. In response, Parliament actually lowered taxes on legally imported tea to undercut this illicit tea trade. The new proposals from the British looked like they were going to deprive some prominent patriots of their livelihood through smuggling tea.
这让许多人感到不满。这种不满最终引发了美国独立战争和一个全新国家的诞生。鉴于过度征税是导致革命的一个巨大因素，早期美国政府在征税时非常谨慎。税收保持在很低的水平，而且极其简单。在许多许多年里，联邦收入主要来自消费税和关税等项目。

8
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:32.000
And it got a lot of people upset. That upset eventually led to the American Revolution and the founding of an entirely new country. Given that tax overreach was a huge contributing factor to the revolution, the early American government was very cautious when it came to taxation. It remained very low and extremely simple. And for many, many decades, federal revenue came mainly from things like excise taxes and tariffs.
为了明确起见，消费税适用于某些商品和服务，比如燃料。如果你不知道什么是关税，好吧，那上帝保佑你，你显然没有关注新闻。在共和国的早期，联邦支出的低足以让国家通过这种方式维持自身运转。直到突然之间，它需要更多的资金。而且要快。在内战期间，和所有战争一样，需要大量的收入。

9
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:58.000
For clarity, excise taxes are applied to certain goods and services, like fuel. And if you don't know what a tariff is, well then, bless your heart, you clearly haven't been looking at the news. In those early years of the republic, federal spending was low enough that the country could fund itself like this. Until suddenly it needed a lot more money. And fast. During the Civil War, like all wars, a lot of revenue was needed.
1862年，林肯总统签署了一项征收所得税的法律以为战争筹措资金。这实际上是美国历史上第一笔联邦所得税，甚至促成了国税局（IRS）的成立，当时被称为国内税收局（Bureau of Internal Revenue）。所得税为联邦政府筹集了5500万美元，相当于今天的约11亿美元。这是一种筹集联邦资金的创新方法。也许对那个时代来说，创新得有点过头了。

10
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:24.000
In 1862, President Lincoln signed into law an income tax to fund the war. This was actually the very first federal income tax in US history, and even led to the creation of the IRS, then called the Bureau of Internal Revenue. The income tax generated $55 million for the Union government, which was the equivalent of about $1.1 billion today. It was an innovative approach to raise federal funds. Maybe a little too innovative for its time.
果不其然，当战争在1872年左右结束时，那项所得税就被取消了。内战算是告一段落后，格兰特政府废除了大多数紧急税收，并恢复了旧的税收征收方式，即消费税，并特别关注所谓的“罪恶税”（SIN taxes）。所有联邦收入的90%来自对烈性酒、啤酒、葡萄酒和烟草的税收。尽管所得税很有效，但领导层仍然对这个想法持抵触态度。

11
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Sure enough, when the war ended, I think, 1872, that income tax was eliminated. With the Civil War behind them, kind of, the Grant administration repealed most emergency taxes and reverted back to the old way of tax collection, excise taxes, with a special focus on what were called SIN taxes. 90% of all federal revenue came from taxes on liquor, beer, wine, and tobacco. Although the income tax was effective, leaders remained resistant to the idea.
消费税似乎足以资助政府，而且它们的实施要简单得多。它们更简单，但许多人认为它们非常不公平。消费税是累退税，这意味着它们给低收入个人带来了更高的负担。如果一个赚很少钱的人和一个赚很多钱的人支付相同的税率，那么他们支付的收入比例就会更高。大多数人会认为这是明显不公平的。因此，岁月流逝，许多人对大部分收入来自烈性酒、啤酒、葡萄酒、烟草税这一事实感到不满，而且这些负担可能主要由低收入人群承担。

12
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Excise taxes seemed adequate to fund the government, plus they were a lot simpler to implement. They were simpler, but a lot of people thought they were very unfair. Excise taxes are regressive, meaning they place a higher burden on lower-income individuals. If someone who makes very little money is paying the same rate as someone who makes a lot of money, then they are paying a higher percentage of their income. And most people would view that as patently unfair. So years go on, and there were a lot of people who were not happy with the fact that most of the revenue is coming from taxes on liquor, beer, wine, tobacco, and probably the burden was felt heavily by lower income people.
因此，从80年代（即18世纪80年代，译者注：此处播客叙述有误，原文指1880年代）开始，尤其是90年代，出现了很多政治支持，要求实行所得税，从而减少这些其他税收。这一切都发生在镀金时代，这是一个贫富差距极大、劳动力阶层遭到剥削的时代。税收负担主要由生活在极度贫困中的绝大多数人承担。与此同时，资本大鳄（robber barons）过着奢华的生活，并与克里斯汀·芭伦斯基（Christine Baranski）等人打成一片。

13
00:06:38.000 --> 00:07:09.000
So picking up in the 80s, that's the 1880s, and especially the 90s, there was a lot of political support for instituting an income tax and therefore reducing these other taxes. This was all taking place during the Gilded Age, an era of profound wealth inequality and exploitation of the labor class. The tax burden was mainly shouldered by the vast majority living in extreme poverty. Meanwhile, robber barons were living in luxury and rubbing elbows with Christine Boranski.
因此，在1894年，在逆境中，国会通过了《威尔逊-戈尔曼关税法》（Wilson-Gorman Tariff Act），实施了有史以来第一笔和平时期的所得税。这项税收专门针对富人，且只适用于任何收入超过4000美元的人，这在当时是收入最高的1%的人口。它还降低了进口关税，使其变得更便宜。因此，该法案在民主党内部赞成自由贸易的一翼中非常受欢迎。但在最高法院却很不受欢迎。

14
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:40.000
So in 1894, against the odds, Congress passed the Wilson-Gorman Tariff Act, which instituted the first-ever peacetime income tax. This tax specifically applied to the rich and only applied to anyone making over $4,000, which at the time was the highest earning 1% of the population. It also lowered tariffs on imports, making them cheaper. So the law was very popular with the pro-free trade wing of the Democratic Party. But unpopular with the Supreme Court.
最高法院裁定所得税违宪，因为，你知道的，较富裕的州的税收负担会比较穷的州更高。因此，基于这一理由，它被裁定为违宪，尽管在制宪会议期间并不清楚什么是直接税，而且直到现在对于当时的意图仍不是完全清楚。好吧，既然它违宪了。那么，如何让联邦所得税不再违宪呢？修改宪法。所得税缓缓通过了批准程序，直到最后在

15
00:07:40.000 --> 00:08:19.000
The Supreme Court had ruled that an income tax was unconstitutional because, you know, richer states, the tax burden would be higher than poorer states. So on that basis, it was ruled unconstitutional, even though it wasn't clear during the Constitutional Convention what a direct tax was, and still isn't entirely clear what was meant. Okay, so it's unconstitutional. And so how do you make a federal income tax not unconstitutional? You change the constitution. The income tax slowly made its way through the ratification process until finally in
1913年，通过第十六修正案将其巩固下来。第十六修正案基本上规定，联邦政府有权征收所得税，即便所征收的税收与人口不成比例。全国性的所得税为联邦政府开辟了一个全新的收入来源，对于第一次缴纳所得税的人来说是一个巨大的改变。但几乎没有人需要报税。豁免门槛如此之高，以至于它实际上被认为是一种阶级税（class tax），而不是大众税（mass tax），因为它适用的范围太小了。

16
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:54.000
1913, it was solidified by the 16th Amendment. And the 16th Amendment basically says the federal government has the right to levy an income tax even though the revenues collected would not be proportional to population. The national income tax opened up the federal government to a whole new source of income and was a huge change for people filing an income tax for the first time. But barely anyone had to file one. The exemption level was so high that it was actually thought of as a class tax, not a mass tax, because it applied to so few people.
因此，在1913年，豁免门槛是3000美元。好吧，这大约相当于当今美元的90,000美元左右。所以在1913年，大约提交了350,000份纳税申报表。那是在一个人口略低于1亿的国家。所得税不仅只影响极小一部分人口，而且其中涉及的 paperwork（文书工作）也简单得令人赞叹。作为一个税务书呆子（tax nerd），你不会惊讶地发现我的办公室里框着一份原始税表的复印件。

17
00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:30.000
So in 1913, the exemption level was $3,000. Okay, that's about $90,000 in today's dollars, approximately. So in 1913, about 350,000 returns were filed. And that's in a country with a population just under 100 million. Not only did the income tax only impact a narrow fraction of the population, the paperwork involved was wonderfully uncomplicated. As a tax nerd, you won't be surprised to know I have a copy of the original tax form framed in my office.
新所得税的申报表格——这就是表格和说明——长达四页。尽管这四页的表格非常简陋，但到了第二年，一些人已经在谴责它的复杂性了。在年度税务会议上，一位发言者宣称：“联邦所得税需要简化，这一点是无可否认的。”

18
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:55.000
The filing form of the new income tax was, and this is the form and the instructions, four pages long. Despite how sparse the four-page form was, by the following year, some were already decrying its complexity. At the annual conference on taxation, one speaker declared, It will hardly be denied that the federal income tax needs simplification.
“它的复杂性是其显著特征。首先，法案所用的语言盘根错节，辞藻令人眼花缭乱。它的术语令人困惑。”但当然，就复杂性而言，此后只会一路走高。短短几十年后，美国迎来了一个彻底改变所得税的转折点。昨天，1941年12月7日，一个遗臭万年的日子。因此，当我们谈论时，显而易见的是，战争在税收的故事中扮演了重要角色。在二战期间，税收发生了非常急剧的变化。

19
00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:42.000
Its complexity is its distinguishing characteristic. To begin with, the language in which the act is couched is involved, and its rhetoric bewildering. Its terminology is confusing. But of course, in terms of complexity, it was only uphill from there. And just a few decades later, the U.S. reached an inflection point that changed the income tax for good. Yesterday, December 7th, 1941, a date which will live in infamy. So as we're talking, it's clear that wars play a big part in the story of taxation. It changed very precipitously in World War II.
战争的绝对规模意味着美国人将面临前所未有的征税。一方面，报税的豁免门槛被大幅降低，这意味着很大一部分人口实际上必须处理它。1939年，提交了770万份申报表，对吧？六年后的数字是4990万。所以这增加了六倍多。这就是巨大的变化。

20
00:10:42.000 --> 00:11:09.000
The sheer scale of the war meant that Americans would be taxed like never before. For one, the exemption level for filing taxes was lowered way down, meaning a lot more of the population actually had to deal with it. In 1939, 7.7 million returns were filed, okay? Six years later, 49.9 million. So that's an increase of more than six times. So that was the big change.
它正式从一种阶级税变成了一种大众税。财政部将其宣传为我们对战争努力的爱国责任去报税。他们甚至委托欧文·柏林（Irving Berlin）为此谱写了一首轻快的曲子。我和美国扯平了。你看到天上的那些轰炸机了吗。洛克菲勒帮忙建造了它们，我也一样。我今天交了所得税。

21
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:37.000
It officially went from being a class tax to a mass tax. And the Department of Treasury plugged it as our patriotic duty to the war effort to file our taxes. They even commissioned Irving Berlin to compose a jaunty little number about it. I'm squared up with the USA. You see those bombers in the sky. Rockefeller helped to build them, so did I. I paid my income tax today.
我今天交了所得税。在这个时期演变的不仅仅是税基。我们征税的方式也发生了改变。1943年之前，个人每年申报一次他们所欠的一笔总税款。理想情况下，纳税人会提前计划并在全年存够钱，以便在春天申报并缴纳税款。但在1943年，联邦政府改变了规则。它说，不，现在雇主必须在你赚钱的同时将钱汇给政府。

22
00:11:37.000 --> 00:12:10.000
I paid my income tax today. And it wasn't just the tax base that evolved during this time. The way we collected them changed as well. Before 1943, individuals filed once a year for the one lump sum that they owed. Ideally, taxpayers would plan ahead and save enough all year in order to file and pay their taxes in the spring. But 1943, the federal government changed the rules. It said, no, now the employers have to be sending money to the government while you're earning it.
如果你作为企业、公司或政府机构的雇员谋生，你知道的，向老板汇报工作，那么你已经完全清楚雇主代扣代缴（employer withholding）制度。法律要求你的雇主从你的工资中扣除一部分，用来缴纳你所欠的税款。这是所得税系统运作方式的一个重大改变。这在几个非常重要的方面使税收征管实现了现代化。首先，大多数公民不再每年一次被巨额税单砸中，因为他们已经通过工资支票按比例分期缴纳了。此外，需要追查的人也变少了。

23
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:47.000
If you earn a living as an employee of a business or a corporation or a government agency, you know, you report to the man, then you are already fully aware of employer withholding. Your employer is required by law to withhold a portion of your wages to pay into taxes you owe. That was a major change in how the income tax system worked. This modernized tax collection in some very important ways. For one, most citizens stopped being slammed by a larger tax bill once a year, since they were already paying into it incrementally through their paychecks. Also, there were fewer people to shake down.
这大大降低了行政负担以及国税局（IRS）的负担。他们不必去追踪所有的雇员。但历史表明，这是税收体系运作方式中最大的创新。世界上几乎所有实行所得税的国家都在这样做。当二战结束、政府开支下降时，你可能会期望所得税再次被废除，就像内战后发生的那样。但相反，它只是留了下来。二战后税收水平并没有急剧下降，有时被称为棘轮效应（ratchet effect）。

24
